  "worked_solution": "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13944892/assets/35797fe2923cdf9c741dda57a40401f4759cd722eb748f80d0df960c5d6eef29.png)\n\nOur trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(21,9)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(25,3).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-9}{25-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6)}{4} \\\\  & =-1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-1.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-1.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(21,9):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1.5x+b \\\\ 9 & =-1.5(21)+b \\\\ 9 & =-31.5+b \\\\ b & =40.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-1.5x+40.5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation above and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1.5x+40.5 \\\\ 0 & =-1.5x+40.5 \\\\ 1.5x & =40.5 \\\\ x & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $y=0$ we have $x\u224827.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=0$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable.",
